Types of Authentication
Password - less sophisticated
Token - This is usually a device or token that temporarily proves your identity. These include One-Time Passwords (OTPs) sent via SMS or generated by an app.
Biometrics - Voice, retina, finger print etc.
Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA) or Two-Factor Authentication (2FA) 

Common Access Control Models
- Discretionary Access Control DAC systems: access is granted by the owner of the object, usually the person who created the file or resource. 
This model is flexible and simple to implement, but it carries risks. Users can unintentionally share sensitive data by granting permissions too broadly or overlooking security implications.
eg. A shared Google Doc where the owner sets who can view or edit the file.
- Mandatory Access Control MAC: a strict, hierarchical model often used in environments where security policies must be enforced without exception, like military or classified government systems. 
Access is based on labels; both users and data are assigned classification levels. Only users with sufficient clearance can access certain resources. 
It’s highly secure but difficult to adapt to fast-changing needs. eg. A classified document that only employees with “Top Secret” clearance can open. 
- Role-based Access Control RBAC: RBAC provides a scalable and maintainable middle ground.
Instead of granting permissions to individual users, administrators create roles (like “Editor” or “Manager”) and assign permissions to those roles. 
Users are then assigned roles based on their responsibilities. It aligns well with organizational structure, making it the most widely adopted model in enterprise systems.
eg.  A project management app where roles like “Admin,” “Contributor,” and “Viewer” determine what users can do.
  The core of RBAC is users, permissions, and roles
In a typical RBAC implementation, administrators assign users to roles, and roles already carry the permissions.
